1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Stargazers saw — a path to find
the stars.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,000
After dark in April, look high in the
northeast.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Follow the curve of its handle. Keep
going in a big arc.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,000
About three fists away: an orange star,
the northern sky’s brightest.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Extend the same arc south, about as far,
to blue-white Spica.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Mizar, Alkaid, Arcturus and Spica lie
almost on one circle.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The astronomy app Stellarium even
includes it as a star-hopping guide.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The Greeks called Arcturus Arktouros —
“guardian of the bear.”

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Northern skies, mid-April, 21:00:
Arcturus in the east, Spica lower in the
southeast.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
India: the Moon’s 27 wives.

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

